.\frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:139:  { text: `The Stanford prison experiment of nineteen seventy-one assigned college students randomly to be guards or prisoners in a simulated prison. Within days, the guards became sadistic — stripping prisoners naked, forcing them into stress positions, denying them sleep. The prisoners broke down emotionally. The experiment was scheduled for two weeks but was terminated after six days. The lesson was not that some people are cruel. The lesson was that ordinary, psychologically healthy people become cruel when placed in a system that rewards cruelty and removes accountability. Evil is less about character and more about context.`, type: 'Ethics', styles: ['documentary', 'dark_psychology'] },
.\frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:197:  { text: `The Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction for over one hundred forty years. Antoni Gaudí began the project in eighteen eighty-two, knowing he would never see it finished. When asked about the slow pace, he said, “My client is not in a hurry” — meaning God. Gaudí spent the last twelve years of his life living on the construction site, sleeping in his workshop, growing increasingly gaunt and obsessive. He was struck by a tram in nineteen twenty-six and died three days later. He was so poorly dressed that passersby assumed he was a beggar. The church is scheduled for completion in twenty twenty-six — the centennial of his death. It will have taken longer to build than the Great Pyramid.`, type: 'History', styles: ['cinematic', 'gothic'] },
.\frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:242:  { text: `In rural Ireland, a postwoman named Siobhan noticed that an elderly farmer always had his kettle on when she arrived, no matter what time she came. For nineteen years, he offered her tea. She always declined — regulations, schedule, rain to beat. On the day she retired, she finally said yes. They sat at his kitchen table for three hours. He told her that his wife had died the year before Siobhan started her route, and that boiling the kettle every morning for "the postwoman who might stay" was the thing that got him out of bed. He died four months later. Siobhan still has the teacup. She never washed it.`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['cinematic', 'vintage_retro'] },
